
{"id":"a7801da2-09b1-4330-b52a-8c2c8eefa2d6","object":"chat.completion","created":1785005211,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    items = list(case['items'])\\n    required = set(case['required_failure_items'])\\n    attempts = 0\\n    g = 2\\n    while True:\\n        if g > len(items):\\n            g = 1\\n        if g < 1:\\n            break\\n        removed = False\\n        for i in range(len(items) - g + 1):\\n            chunk = items[i:i+g]\\n            attempts += 1\\n            new_items = items[:i] + items[i+g:]\\n            if required.issubset(set(new_items)):\\n                items = new_items\\n                removed = True\\n                break\\n        if removed:\\n            g += 1\\n        else:\\n            if g == 1:\\n                break\\n            g *= 2\\n    one_minimal = True\\n    for idx in range(len(items)):\\n        if items[idx] not in required:\\n            candidate = items[:idx] + items[idx+1:]\\n            if required.issubset(set(candidate)):\\n                one_minimal = False\\n                break\\n    return {\\n        'attempts': attempts,\\n        'one_minimal': one_minimal,\\n        'reduced_items': items\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-CR-02. From the fixture payload, case has items (list of strings) and required_failure_items (list of strings). Also _case_nonce but ignore.\n\nThe task: chunk reduction schedule. Starting granularity 2. Try chunks from left to right, accept removal if required failure items remain (i.e., after removal, the set of required failure items is still a subset of the remaining items? Or \"remain\" means all required failure items are still present? The description: \"Apply deterministic chunk-removal and granularity-refinement passes while preserving a supplied syntax and interestingness oracle.\" But we don't have an oracle; we have required_failure_items. It says \"accept removal only if required failure items remain.\" That likely means that after removing a chunk, all required_failure_items are still present in the remaining items. So we need to ensure that none of the required failure items are removed.\n\nWe start with granularity=2. Try chunks from left to right: at each step, consider removing a contiguous chunk of size granularity. If after removing that chunk, all required_failure_items are still in the list, then accept removal (i.e., remove that chunk). After an accepted removal, restart with slightly coarser granularity? \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" That suggests after a successful removal, we increase granularity? Or \"slightly coarser\" means increase granularity step? Actually: \"Start chunk removal at granularity two. Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\"\n\nInterpretation:\n\n- Initial granularity = 2.\n- At each granularity, we try to remove chunks of that size from left to right. For each chunk, if removing it keeps all required_failure_items present, we remove it (update the list) and then restart the process from the beginning? \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" means after a successful removal, we increase granularity? But it says \"slightly coarser\" maybe by 1? Then later: \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" So there are two modes: after a successful removal, we increase granularity by some amount (coarser, but not double?), and when no removal succeeds at a given granularity, we double it. And we stop when granularity becomes 1? \"stop at singleton granularity\" means when granularity becomes 1, we stop? Or when it becomes less than 2? Actually: \"and stop at singleton granularity.\" That suggests we continue until granularity equals 1, then stop.\n\nBut we need a precise algorithm. Let's reason:\n\nWe have a list of items. We want to reduce it by removing chunks while preserving required failure items. This is a kind of delta debugging. The algorithm described seems like: Start with chunk size 2. Try all possible contiguous chunks of that size. For each chunk in order, if removing it is safe (all required items still present), then actually remove it and then increase granularity by some factor? \"slightly coarser\" might mean adding 1? But then \"double granularity when no removal succeeds\" suggests after trying all chunks of current size, if none succeeded, we double the chunk size. And we stop when chunk size becomes 1? Singleton granularity means size 1? But we start at 2, doubling would go 2,4,8,... and stop when size becomes 1? That never reaches 1. Perhaps \"singleton granularity\" means granularity = 1, i.e., individual items. That could be reached by doubling? No, doubling 2 gives 4, not 1. Alternatively, \"slightly coarser\" could mean increase by 1, so after successful removal, granularity becomes 3, 4, etc. And when no removal succeeds at a given granularity, we double? Then we might skip some values.\n\nLet's look at the fixture cases:\n\nCase 0: items t0-t9, required_failure_items: [\"t0\",\"t9\"]. Starting granularity 2. Chunks of size 2: [t0,t1] - removing would remove t0, which is required, so not accepted. Next chunk [t1,t2] - safe? t0 and t9 still present? Yes, t1 and t2 not required. So we can remove [t1,t2]. Then we restart with slightly coarser granularity? So after removal, new list: t0, t3, t4, t5, t6, t7, t8, t9. Now granularity? \"slightly coarser\" might be granularity+1=3? Then try chunks of size 3: from left: [t0,t3,t4] - removes t0, fail. [t3,t4,t5] - safe? required still t0 and t9? t0 still present, t9 still present. So remove [t3,t4,t5]. Then new list: t0, t6, t7, t8, t9. Granularity becomes 4? Then chunk size 4: [t0,t6,t7,t8] - removes t0, fail. [t6,t7,t8,t9] - removes t9, fail. No removal succeeds. Then we double granularity -> 8. But size 8 from list of 5? Not possible. Maybe we double until granularity exceeds list length, then stop? \"stop at singleton granularity\" might mean stop when granularity becomes 1? Not clear.\n\nAnother interpretation: The algorithm might be a variant of delta debugging: Start with granularity 2. Test each chunk. If a chunk removal fails (i.e., removes a required item), we ignore. If a chunk removal succeeds, we accept it and then continue with the same granularity but restart from the beginning? The description says \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" meaning increase granularity. Then \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" meaning after finishing all chunks at current granularity with no success, double it. And \"stop at singleton granularity\" might mean stop when granularity becomes 1 (i.e., individual items) after having doubled? But starting from 2, doubling gives 4, not 1. Unless we also consider decreasing? Hmm.\n\nPerhaps \"singleton granularity\" refers to granularity = 1 (i.e., removing individual items). But we start at 2. So we might need to eventually try granularity 1. But the algorithm says double when no success, which would never hit 1. Maybe \"slightly coarser\" means increase by 1, and \"double\" is separate. Then after successful removals, granularity increases by 1; after no success, granularity doubles. And we stop when granularity becomes 1? That would happen if we start at 2 and after a few increments we might get to something, but then doubling could overshoot. Alternatively, \"stop at singleton granularity\" could mean we stop when the current granularity is greater than or equal to the length of the current list? No.\n\nLet's think about typical delta debugging: Start with chunk size = len(items)/2, then test, if fails, reduce chunk size, etc. But here it's reversed: start small and increase.\n\nGiven the fixture cases, we can deduce expected output by reasoning about the algorithm. But we don't have expected outputs. However, the task says \"Return the reduced items, attempt count, and an explicit one-minimal check.\" So we need to compute reduced_items, attempts (number of times we tested a removal?), and one_minimal (boolean indicating if the final result is one-minimal, meaning no single item can be removed while preserving required items).\n\nWe need to implement the algorithm as described.\n\nLet's parse the description carefully:\n\n\"Start chunk removal at granularity two. Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\"\n\nInterpretation:\n\n- We have a current granularity g. Initially g=2.\n- While g >= 1? Or while g <= len(items)? \"Stop at singleton granularity\" means stop when g becomes 1? But starting at 2, we only get to 1 if we decrease? Not described. Maybe \"singleton granularity\" means granularity of 1 item (i.e., individual). So we should eventually try g=1. How do we get there? After no removal succeeds at a certain g, we double g. That increases. So we might never reach g=1. Unless after doubling, if it exceeds the list length, we then set g=1? Or maybe \"stop at singleton granularity\" means we stop when the list length becomes 1? That doesn't match.\n\nAlternative: Perhaps \"singleton granularity\" refers to granularity = 1, meaning we only consider removing single items. And the algorithm goes: Start at granularity 2, try all chunks. If success, restart with g+1. If no success, then check if g is 1? If not, set g = g*2? But when would g become 1? Not from doubling. Could be that \"double granularity\" means after no success, we reduce granularity? No, double means increase.\n\nMaybe the algorithm is a variant of \"chunk reduction\" where we iterate granularities from 2 upward, and after a successful removal we continue with the same granularity but recalculate? Hmm.\n\nLet's look at the public fixture: only three cases, all with 10 items and two required items at ends or middle. The algorithm likely yields different reduced lists.\n\nWe might need to implement a deterministic algorithm that matches expected outputs. Since we don't have expected outputs, we must infer from the description. Possibly the intended algorithm is:\n\n- Start g = 2.\n- While g <= len(items):\n  - For each chunk of size g from left to right:\n    - If removing that chunk keeps all required items, then remove it, update items, and break (restart with increased granularity).\n  - If no chunk was removed at this granularity:\n    - If g == 1: break (stop)\n    - Else: g = g * 2\n  - Else (if a chunk was removed):\n    - g = g + 1? Or g = g * 2? \"slightly coarser\" suggests +1, but \"restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" means after removal, we increase granularity a bit. Then \"double granularity when no removal succeeds\" means when we fail to remove any chunk at current g, we double. This is plausible.\n\nBut \"stop at singleton granularity\" would then be: we stop when g becomes 1? How does that happen? If we double enough, g might exceed list length and then we never get to 1. Perhaps after the doubling step, if g becomes 1? No. Maybe \"singleton granularity\" means g=1, and we treat it as a special case: when g is doubled and becomes 1? That's impossible. Or maybe \"stop at singleton granularity\" means we stop when the list has only 1 item? Not.\n\nAnother possibility: The algorithm is symmetric: we try to remove chunks, and after each removal we go back to granularity 2? \"Restart after an accepted removal\" could mean reset to granularity 2. Then \"slightly coarser\" is ambiguous. But then \"double granularity when no removal succeeds\" makes no sense because you'd always restart at 2.\n\nI think I need to look at the task scaffold: \"Implement `chunk_reduction_schedule` through `solve(case)` for this bounded objective: Apply deterministic chunk-removal and granularity-refinement passes while preserving a supplied syntax and interestingness oracle.\" The \"interestingness oracle\" is the required_failure_items remaining. So we are essentially performing a variant of the delta debug algorithm, but with a specific schedule.\n\nMaybe the algorithm is exactly the one described in the \"candidate_artifact\" section: \"Start chunk removal at granularity two. Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\"\n\nLet's assume \"slightly coarser\" means increment by 1, and \"double\" means multiply by 2. And \"stop at singleton granularity\" means stop when granularity becomes 1. But how do we reach 1? Suppose after a successful removal, we increment g. g might increase. After no success, we double. Eventually g might become > len(items). At that point, we could set g = 1? Or we could stop. The phrase \"stop at singleton granularity\" might mean that we continue until we have tried g=1, which is the finest granularity. So we need to eventually try g=1. How do we get to g=1 from starting at 2? Only by decrementing, which is not described. Perhaps after no removal at a given g and after doubling, if the doubled g exceeds the current list length, we then set g=1? That seems arbitrary.\n\nAnother interpretation: \"singleton granularity\" means granularity of 1, i.e., removing single items. And the algorithm includes a final pass with g=1 after all other passes. That is, after the loop that doubles, we finish with a pass at g=1. The description says \"and stop at singleton granularity\" which could imply that the last granularity we try is singleton (g=1). So the algorithm: start g=2, while True: try chunks of size g, if success, increment g (coarser) and restart? Actually \"restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" – that suggests after a removal, we go to next granularity (g+1) and continue from the beginning of the list. And when no removal succeeds at a given g, we double g and then if g becomes 1? No.\n\nLet's try to simulate the first fixture with a plausible algorithm to see what result might make sense.\n\nCase 0: items [t0..t9], required [t0,t9].\nGoal: reduce while preserving t0,t9.\n\nIf we start g=2, chunk [t0,t1] fails, [t1,t2] succeeds -> remove [t1,t2]. Now list: t0,t3,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9. Then \"restart with slightly coarser granularity\" -> g=3. Now try chunks size 3: [t0,t3,t4] fails, [t3,t4,t5] succeeds -> remove [t3,t4,t5]. List: t0,t6,t7,t8,t9. g increments to 4? Try chunks size 4: [t0,t6,t7,t8] fails, [t6,t7,t8,t9] fails. No success. Then \"double granularity\" -> g=8. But list length is 5, chunks of size 8 not possible. So maybe we skip. Then what? \"stop at singleton granularity\" -> maybe now we try g=1. Try chunks of size 1 from left: [t0] fails, [t6] succeeds? t6 is not required, so removing t6 preserves t0,t9. So we remove t6. Then restart with slightly coarser granularity? g becomes 2? That seems messy.\n\nAlternatively, after doubling to 8, since no chunk fits, we might then set g=1 and try individual items. That could yield final reduced list: t0,t7,t8,t9 after removing t6? But then continue? The algorithm description is open to interpretation.\n\nGiven that the output schema requires reduced_items (array of strings), attempts (integer), and one_minimal (boolean). The one_minimal check likely means after the algorithm finishes, check if the reduced list is one-minimal: i.e., no single item can be removed without losing a required failure item. So we need to compute that.\n\nWe need to be precise. Perhaps the intent is to implement a variant of the \"ddmin\" algorithm from delta debugging, but with a specific chunk schedule. Actually, the standard ddmin algorithm starts with granularity = len/2, then halves when a removal fails, etc. Here it's reversed. But the description is explicit.\n\nI think the safest approach is to implement exactly as described with the following interpretation:\n\n- Set items = list(case['items']) (copy)\n- Set required = set(case['required_failure_items'])\n- g = 2\n- attempts = 0\n- While g <= len(items) and g >= 1: (we stop when g becomes 1 and we've already tried it? But need to handle singleton)\n  - For i from 0 to len(items)-g:\n    - chunk = items[i:i+g]\n    - attempts += 1\n    - if after removing chunk, required items still all present (using set), then remove chunk from items, and break out of for loop.\n  - If a chunk was removed:\n    - g = g + 1   (slightly coarser)\n    - continue while loop (restart scanning from left)\n  - Else:\n    - if g == 1: break (stop because we already tried singleton and no removal succeeded)\n    - else: g = g * 2\n- After loop, reduced_items = items.\n- one_minimal = True if for every single item in reduced_items, removing it would cause some required item to be missing. (i.e., test each item: if item not required and removing it still leaves all required, then not one-minimal)\n- Return {\"attempts\": attempts, \"one_minimal\": one_minimal, \"reduced_items\": reduced_items}\n\nBut we need to ensure we handle the case where g becomes > len(items) after doubling. Should we then set g = 1? Or break? The phrase \"stop at singleton granularity\" could mean that after doubling, if g > len(items), we then set g = len(items) or 1? I'll assume that after doubling, we cap g to len(items) if it exceeds, but then we would try g = len(items) which is the whole list, but that would remove all items and fail. That doesn't make sense.\n\nMaybe \"stop at singleton granularity\" means we stop when we have tried g=1. So we need to ensure we eventually try g=1. So after the while loop, if g!=1, we should also try g=1. But the algorithm described might naturally lead to g=1 when doubling? No.\n\nLet's look at the case when g is doubled and becomes > len(items). At that point, we could set g = 1 (singleton). And then run the algorithm one more time with g=1 (i.e., try removing each single item). That would make sense: \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity\" could be interpreted as: after doubling, if the new granularity is greater than the current number of items, then set granularity to 1. And then run again. And after that, we stop.\n\nLet's test with case 0 after removing [t1,t2] and [t3,t4,t5], we have list length 5, g=4, no removal succeeds. Double g to 8, which >5, so set g=1. Then try g=1: scan from left: [t0] fails, [t6] succeeds -> remove t6. Then after removal, list length 4, g becomes 2? \"restart with slightly coarser granularity\" after a removal, g = 1+1=2. Now list: t0,t7,t8,t9, g=2. Try chunks size 2: [t0,t7] fails, [t7,t8] succeeds? t7,t8 not required, remove them -> list: t0,t9. g becomes 3. But list length 2, g=3 >2, so try g=1? Actually after removal, we increment g, then next iteration we try chunks of g=3, but list length is 2, so no possible chunks, so no removal succeeds. Then double g: 3*2=6 >2, set g=1. Try g=1: [t0] fails, [t9] fails. Stop. Final reduced: [t0,t9]. attempts count would include many attempts.\n\nThis seems plausible. The algorithm reduces to the minimal set containing required items, which is expected. So one_minimal is true.\n\nFor case 1: required [\"t1\",\"t4\"]. Let's simulate quickly with same algorithm:\n\nStart items: t0..t9, g=2:\n- chunks: [t0,t1] fails because t1 is required.\n- [t1,t2] fails because removes t1.\n- [t2,t3] safe? t1 and t4 remain. Remove t2,t3. List: t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9. g=3.\n- g=3 chunks: [t0,t1,t4] fails (removes t1 and t4), [t1,t4,t5] fails (removes t1,t4), [t4,t5,t6] fails (removes t4), [t5,t6,t7] safe? t1 and t4 remain. Remove [t5,t6,t7]. List: t0,t1,t4,t8,t9. g=4.\n- g=4 chunks: [t0,t1,t4,t8] fails, [t1,t4,t8,t9] fails. No success. g=8 (>6) -> set g=1.\n- g=1: [t0] safe? Remove t0. List: t1,t4,t8,t9. g=2.\n- g=2: [t1,t4] fails, [t4,t8] fails (removes t4), [t8,t9] safe? Remove t8,t9. List: t1,t4. g=3.\n- g=3 > len=2, no chunks, no success, double to 6>2, set g=1.\n- g=1: [t1] fails, [t4] fails. Stop. Reduced: [t1,t4]. one_minimal true.\n\nCase 2: required [\"t1\",\"t6\"].\nStart: items t0..t9, g=2:\n- [t0,t1] fails, [t1,t2] fails (removes t1), [t2,t3] safe? Remove [t2,t3]. List: t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9. g=3.\n- g=3: [t0,t1,t4] fails, [t1,t4,t5] fails (removes t1), [t4,t5,t6] fails (removes t6), [t5,t6,t7] fails (removes t6), [t6,t7,t8] fails (removes t6), [t7,t8,t9] safe? Remove [t7,t8,t9]. List: t0,t1,t4,t5,t6. g=4.\n- g=4: [t0,t1,t4,t5] fails, [t1,t4,t5,t6] fails. No success. g=8 >5 -> set g=1.\n- g=1: [t0] safe? Remove t0. List: t1,t4,t5,t6. g=2.\n- g=2: [t1,t4] fails, [t4,t5] safe? Remove [t4,t5]. List: t1,t6. g=3.\n- g=3 >2, no success, double to 6>2, set g=1.\n- g=1: [t1] fails, [t6] fails. Stop. Reduced: [t1,t6]. one_minimal true.\n\nSo this algorithm yields reduced items equal to required_failure_items in all three cases. That seems plausible.\n\nNow, we need to implement solve(case) that returns the above. Also count attempts: each time we consider a chunk and test, we increment attempts. The final one_minimal is true as above.\n\nBut we must also handle case where required items are not all present initially? Probably the input guarantees that. Also if some required items are duplicates? Not specified.\n\nWe must ensure we do not mutate case. So we copy items list.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- items = list(case['items'])\n- required_set = set(case['required_failure_items'])\n- attempts = 0\n- g = 2\n- while True:\n    if g > len(items):\n        # set to singleton granularity? Actually according to plan, when g exceeds len, set to 1.\n        g = 1\n    if g < 1:\n        break\n    removed = False\n    for i in range(len(items)-g+1):\n        # Consider removing items[i:i+g]\n        chunk = items[i:i+g]\n        attempts += 1\n        # Check if required_set is subset of items without chunk\n        new_items = items[:i] + items[i+g:]\n        if required_set.issubset(set(new_items)):\n            # Accept removal\n            items = new_items\n            removed = True\n            break\n    if removed:\n        g += 1  # slightly coarser\n    else:\n        if g == 1:\n            break\n        g *= 2\n        # If after doubling g > len(items), next iteration will set g=1 if we implement that logic.\n        # But we need to handle that: we can simply continue; the condition at top will set g=1 if > len.\n        # However, we risk infinite loop if we keep doubling and then resetting to 1, then doubling again? Let's think:\n        # After we set g=1 because >len, we run with g=1. If no removal, we break because g==1. So it's fine.\n        # If removal happens at g=1, we increment to 2 and continue, not infinite.\n\nBut careful: When we double g after no removal, if g becomes > len, the next loop iteration will set g=1. That is fine. However, there is a potential issue: after setting g=1 and no removal, we break. But what if after doubling, g becomes exactly len? Then we try chunks of that size (the whole list). That would remove all items if safe, but likely fails because required items are there. So no removal, then we double again, which will be > len, then set to 1, then try 1, then break.\n\nEdge cases: If the initial list is such that required items are already minimal, the algorithm should stop quickly.\n\nWe also need to count attempts correctly. Each time we consider a chunk, we increment attempts, even if we decide not to remove. That matches the test.\n\nNow, one_minimal: After the algorithm finishes, we check if the reduced items list is one-minimal: f